encyclopedia :glass_burrower do
  name "Glass Burrower"
  kind :lifeform
  subkind :creature
  status :complete
  topics :ecology, :ringglass, :danger, :mystery
  prevalence :rare
  appears_when all: { place: [:ringglass_rich] }
  summary "Glass burrowers are deep-living creatures that tunnel through solid ringglass by softening it ahead of themselves, leaving flute-smooth bores that ring when wind crosses them. A working burrower is heard for seasons at a time and sighted once in a surveyor's career, and glass country maps its bores like caves."
  origin "Native to the deep glass, by the survey consensus; the ring's registers record the bores and stay silent on the borer"
  biology "A softener-borer of the deep sheets — the bore walls anneal glassy behind it, and what the softening is remains the deep surveys' standing prize"
  resonance_relation "Softens ringglass ahead of itself by a mechanism instruments register as a moving cold spot in the structural band"

  descriptive_identity(
    appearance: "The certain sightings agree on a segmented, mole-blunt body longer than a " \
                "person, glass-gray and slow, with a head cluster of pale feelers held " \
                "against the working face. The bores agree with each other better than the " \
                "sightings do.",
    behavior: "It works deep, slow, and alone, softening a body-width tunnel through solid " \
              "glass and annealing the walls smooth behind it. Working sound carries " \
              "through the sheet — a patient grinding hum crews learn to sleep through " \
              "and wake to changes in.",
    threat: "The creature avoids voids, warmth, and company with what reads as intent; the " \
            "danger is its work — a new bore under a foundation rewrites the sheet's " \
            "strength, and glass country treats fresh burrower sound the way span " \
            "listeners treat a new note.",
    senses: "It turns away from active resonance work — cutting, singing, heavy tuning — " \
            "days before the work arrives, and the deep crews use singers as repellent " \
            "on that observation alone.",
    risks: "Old bores honeycomb the deep sheets invisibly, and the flute-song of wind " \
           "across their mouths is the only free map — a silent sheet is either unbored " \
           "or holding its breath, and the surveys charge accordingly."
  )

  prose <<~PROSE
    Deep in the great sheets — under Avar, in the Kyther roots, through the thick fused masses of the Shear — something tunnels through solid ringglass, and the glass country has organized a working relationship with it sight unseen. The glass burrower softens the sheet ahead of itself by a mechanism the instruments register as a moving cold spot in the structural band, passes through at a patient grinding pace, and anneals the bore smooth behind it: flute-perfect tunnels, body-wide, that honeycomb the old glass for spans in every direction. Wind across a bore field sings, each mouth its own note, and the bore-song of a ridge on a moving evening is glass country's night music and its survey data in one.

    The creature itself is the deep glass's most productive absence. The certain sightings since the Glassfall fit in a thin folder — segmented, mole-blunt, longer than a person, feelers at the working face — while the bores number in the tens of thousands and the working sound is common enough that sheet settlements distinguish their resident's hum from a stranger's. The working relationship rests on two observed reliabilities: burrowers avoid voids, warmth, and each other, and they turn away from active resonance work days before it reaches them. Deep crews accordingly cut with a singer working the faces in rotation, foundations in bore country are sunk with a season of loud tuning first, and the practice's plain success is cited by both sides of the standing argument about how much the creature understands.
  PROSE

  cue "Through the floor of the deep gallery comes a patient grinding hum, slightly nearer than last month, and the crew notes it in the log under the resident's name."
  cue "Evening wind crosses the ridge and the old bore field sings — dozens of flute notes, each mouth its own — and the surveyor on the team sketches the chord."
  affordance "The bores are ready-made deep access anywhere the sheets are old — smooth, stable, annealed passages the surveys map by ear from the wind-song — and the creature's known aversions make loud tuning a reliable fence for foundations and cuts."
  pressure "The work rewrites sheet strength invisibly and the worker declines to be found, so every deep project in glass country carries a bore survey, a listening watch, and a budget line for the day the hum changes direction."
  variation "Sheet settlements name their resident burrowers and track their hums across generations; the Shear's fused masses carry bores of a gauge beyond anything on the surface, and the claim crews argue about what that implies over drinks."
  variation "Bore-song mapping is a real surveying trade — mouths chorded by ear on moving evenings, charts sold like soundings — and its practitioners' feud with instrument survey is old, public, and mostly performance."
end
